**Concern.** The title group `(?:\*\*.*?\*\*)?` can end at any later `**`. When the tail `.*?[.!?](?:\*\*)?(?=\s|$)` fails, V8's backtracking engine tries each of those ends in turn, and each try rescans the rest of the text. On a text with many `**` and no sentence end that is O(n²). The old `/^.*?[.!?](?:\*\*)?(?=\s|$)/` was O(n): it is anchored and has a single lazy scan.

```ts
function firstSentence(text: string): string {
  return /^(?:\*\*.*?\*\*)?.*?[.!?](?:\*\*)?(?=\s|$)/.exec(text)?.[0] ?? text;
}
```

**Trigger.** ADR 0183's *Ceiling* says a session can rewrite a checklist for a later compile, because the main checkout is within its shell reach (decision 46). Say a session writes a one-line paragraph above the first `##` in `.claude/agents/review-correctness.md`: `**` followed by about 1,048,000 `*`. That fits under `readPlainFile`'s 1 MiB cap (`src/adapters/capability.utils.ts`). `rubricLines` keeps the paragraph because it starts with `**`, and `sanitizeReason`'s 300-char cap runs only *after* `firstSentence`, so the regex gets the whole MiB.

**Wrong result.** That is about n²/2 ≈ 5×10^11 backtracking steps. While `rework/shift-left-kickoff` is `on`, `reviewersSection` runs on every worker compile: session launch and `recompileSessionProfile` in `pup fix-round`. So every later launch and fix round for the project pins a core for minutes, other sessions' launches included, until someone repairs the file. Decision 183 accepts that a session can get a sentence through. It does not accept a pup hang, and the 1 MiB cap in `readPlainFile` exists to bound exactly this kind of work.

**Fix.** Give the title exactly one possible end, which matches the new test's own `\*\*[^*]+\*\*`. `**Title.** Body. More.` still gives `**Title.** Body.`.

